A('')
A('## Effects on the Economy and on Households')
A('')
A('The ledger uses conventional scoring, as CBO\'s cost estimates do: it does not add any boost or drag from the agenda\'s effect on the wider economy. Those effects are real and run both ways.')
A('')
A('- **Borrowing.** Added debt raises interest costs and can crowd out private investment. CBO\'s dynamic analysis found the 2025 tax law\'s effects on the economy *increased* its cost [Source 337]. This agenda\'s smaller deficit and revenue-first sequence limit that drag, but do not remove it.')
A('- **Work and investment.** CBO found that expanding subsidized child care would raise parents\' participation in the labor force [Source 814]; paid leave keeps workers attached to jobs; infrastructure spending has measurable short-run effects [Source 28]. Higher taxes on top wealth and capital income lower saving at the top.')
A('- **Who pays and who gains.** The revenue lines fall on the highest-income and highest-wealth households; the largest costs are credits and benefits aimed at families below about three times the poverty line. The direction is clearly progressive. Quantified distribution tables, by income group and household type in the style of CBO, the Tax Policy Center, and the Netherlands Bureau for Economic Policy Analysis [Sources 330, 334, 935], require a microsimulation model and are the next step before any bill is introduced.')
A('')
A('## Five Conclusions')
A('')
A(f"1. **The agenda costs less than recent choices.** Its central effect is {up(FC['total'])} over ten years with interest. The 2025 tax law adds $4.1 trillion by CBO\'s count, over an earlier window. All 20,000 simulated outcomes for this agenda fall below that.")
A(f"2. **It is not deficit-neutral, and the gap grows.** The yearly effect reaches about {T(ann39)} by 2039. Four provisions keep it in check without cutting a benefit â€” a self-financing paid-leave contribution, two Medicare payment-accuracy offsets, a 23% corporate rate, and a pay-as-you-go limit on the Phase II family programs â€” and AIPE Fund returns are the next lever.")
A(f"3. **The revenue side is the largest uncertainty, stated rather than hidden.** The billionaire income tax is counted at a risk-weighted value; if its annual inclusion is struck down and only the bill\'s fallback survives, the agenda adds {T(BTD)}. If every uncertain revenue line fails, it adds {T(FAIL)}.")
A('4. **Interest rates now matter more than any single proposal.** Rates 0.5 point above CBO\'s path add $1.9 trillion to the nation\'s deficits through 2036. That makes the revenue-first sequence a fiscal requirement, not a preference.')
A('5. **Social Security is financed in its own ledger.** Applying the payroll tax to earnings above $250,000 closes about 57% of the long-range gap [Source 810]; it is never used to offset general spending.')
A('')
A('### The Durability Contrast')
A('')
A('|Dimension|This Agenda|Project 2025|Why|')
A('|---|---|---|---|')
A(f'|Legislative development|Advanced drafts; not introduction-ready|High|Sixteen bills and five drafted standalone vehicles with one aligned sequencing map; {tbd_count()} amounts, rates, thresholds, and dates remain marked [TBD] for legislative counsel and CBO.|')
A('|Executive readiness|Developing|High|Methodology and position profiles complete; the vetted-personnel database is the remaining gap, stated plainly.|')
A('|Political durability|High|Low|Policy stickiness by design; vulnerable executive programs are written into statute.|')
A('|Fiscal credibility|High|Low|Line-item ledger built to scorekeeper rules, with a published model anyone can rerun; every simulated outcome falls below the comparator\'s enacted cost.|')
A('')
A('*Ratings are this agenda\'s own assessment, based on the evidence presented in Parts VI and VII; readers can test them against the sources in Part XI.*')
A('')
A('## Working Values for Amounts the Bills Leave Open')
A('')
A(f"The bills mark {tbd_count()} amounts, rates, thresholds, and dates as [TBD] for legislative counsel and CBO. About fifteen of them drive the numbers in this Part. The values below are the ones this review assumes, so the bills and the ledger describe the same policy. Counsel and CBO may change any of them; if they do, the inputs file changes and every table is rebuilt.")
A('')
A('|Bill and section|Open amount|Working value used in this Part|Basis|')
A('|---|---|---|---|')
for r_ in (
 ('COLT Act Secs. 201â€“202','Childcare credit and supply grants','Costs capped at 7% of income for households up to about 300% of the poverty line; supply grants at the scale of the 2021 design','Half the child care cost CBO scored for that design [Sources 925, 926]'),
 ('COLT Act Sec. 401','Effective date of restored premium credits','Tax years beginning after December 31, 2029','First plan year after enactment'),
 ('COLT Act Sec. 403','Rural Health Transformation funding','$10 billion a year','Current-law level [Source 454]'),
 ('COLT Act Sec. 404(c)','EMS stabilization grants','$0.5 billion a year','Assumption'),
 ('COLT Act Sec. 501','Apprenticeship grants and credit','About $0.9 billion a year in total','National Apprenticeship Act analog [Source 941]'),
 ('COLT Act Sec. 502','Teacher salary floor and grants','$60,000 floor; grants reaching $15 billion a year by FY2032','Gap-filling grants only; see the ledger note'),
 ('ECFA Sec. 101','Starting wage floor','$17 an hour, reached over four years, then indexed','The level CBO scored in 2023 [Source 816]'),
 ('FSW Act Sec. 101','Near-universal childcare expansion','7% cap extended to nearly all families, as in the 2021 design','The other half of the CBO-based cost'),
 ('FSW Act Sec. 501','Children\'s account contributions','Up to $2,000 a year at or below the poverty line, phasing to zero at 500% of poverty','Booker-scale design [Source 942]'),
 ('WFA Sec. 202','Paid leave weeks and benefit','12 weeks; replacement rate and weekly cap set so total benefits match the FAMILY Act design (two-thirds of wages up to about $1,000 a week)','CBO\'s FAMILY Act estimate [Source 815]'),
 ('WFA Sec. 203','Reserve and rate ceiling','Six months of benefits in reserve; contribution ceiling of 1% of wages','Covers the program with a margin'),
 ('BWDS Act Sec. 101','Digital services levy','3% on U.S. digital revenue of companies with more than $1 billion in global revenue and $50 million in U.S. digital revenue','Scaled from the UK and French levies [Source 943]'),
 ('BWDS Act Sec. 201(e)','Rate above the capital gains rate','None in the central case; up to the ordinary income rate in the high case','The design JCT scored [Source 930]'),
 ('IIC Act Titles III, V, VI','Air traffic control, water, and preparedness funding','$30 billion over five years; $9.8 billion a year; $1 billion a year','See the ledger notes [Source 823]'),
 ('TSTR Act Title I','Indian Health Service funding','Set in tribal consultation; the ledger\'s even phase-in to the workgroup\'s figure is illustrative only','[Source 827]')):
    A('|' + '|'.join(r_) + '|')
A('')
A('## Reproducing These Numbers')
A('')
A('Every figure in this Part is produced by the ARA Fiscal Model. Its files are attached to the PDF edition of this document (open the attachments panel in a PDF reader) and travel with it as a companion file set: a short Python program (`fiscal_model.py`), a single inputs file (`ara_fiscal_inputs.json`) holding every amount, factor, and source number, and a workbook of the results (`ARA_FiscalModel_2026.xlsx`). With Python and the numpy, scipy, and openpyxl packages installed, running `python3 fiscal_model.py` and then `python3 gen_part6.py` rebuilds every table and figure in this Part from the inputs.')
A('')
A('|Element|Rule or value|')
A('|---|---|')
A('|Adjusting an older estimate|Yearly amount = (source total Ã· years in the source window) Ã— adjustment factors Ã— (1 + growth rate) ^ (year âˆ’ middle of the source window) Ã— ramp-up share for that year|')
gr = fm.META['growth']
A(f"|Growth rates|GDP {gr['gdp']*100:.1f}% Â· wages {gr['wages']*100:.1f}% Â· health costs {gr['health']*100:.1f}% Â· childcare costs {gr['childcare']*100:.1f}% Â· capital income and wealth {gr['capital']*100:.1f}% Â· clean-energy build-out {gr['build']*100:.1f}% a year|")
A(f"|Interest|Interest in year t = r Ã— (added debt through year t âˆ’ 1 + Â½ Ã— change in the deficit in year t); added debt includes earlier interest. r = {fm.META['debt_rate']*100:.1f}%, a blend of CBO\'s projected 4.3% 10-year rate and lower short-term rates [Sources 800, 922]|")
A('|Revenue stacking order|IRS enforcement â†’ loophole closures â†’ billionaire income tax (Ã—0.9 for overlap) â†’ public option savings|')
A('|Legal-risk weight|Billionaire income tax: an even (0.5) chance the annual inclusion is upheld; if not, the Sec. 204(f) fallback keeps 40% of revenue in the window (central factor 0.70; low case 0.4, fallback only; high case 1.3, upheld at a higher rate)|')
A('|Pay-as-you-go limit|In each year, gated cost = the lesser of (childcare expansion + children\'s accounts) and (billionaire income tax + loophole closures + digital levy)|')
A(f"|Simulation|Triangular distribution per line over (low, central, high); revenue lines that depend on enforcement and courts correlated at 0.6, family take-up lines at 0.5; {fm.META['mc_draws']:,} draws, fixed seed {fm.META['mc_seed']}|")
A('|GDP for debt ratios|About $45 trillion in 2036, implied by CBO\'s higher-rate scenario ($1.9 trillion of added debt equals 4.2 points of GDP) [Source 922], grown 4% a year after 2036|')
A('|Data vintages|CBO outlook, February 2026 Â· CBO tariff update, August 20, 2026 Â· CBO rules of thumb, April 2026 Â· CBO higher-rate scenarios, October 8, 2026 Â· Social Security and Medicare Trustees, 2026 Â· FY2026 Treasury results, October 16, 2026 Â· Treasury yields, early November 2026|')
A('|Update rule|When CBO issues a new baseline, or CBO or JCT scores any bill in this agenda, the inputs file is updated and every table is rebuilt; an official score replaces any analog. Published versions are kept with a record of what changed.|')
A('')
A('-----')
A('')
out = '\n'.join(P)
open(os.path.join(OD, 'part6.md'), 'w').write(out)
print('written', len(out.split()), 'words')

endstream
