id,proposal,label,block,side,category,phase,group,low,central,high,central_cbo_window,uncertainty,unc_detail,sources,basis
P15a,P15,"Childcare credit, first stage (COLT Act Title II)",core,cost,Mandatory,I,Family and children,270.7,361.0,487.3,228.0,High,Data M Â· Model M Â· Behaviour H,814;925;926,"CBO: $752B for a permanent BBB child care and pre-K title, 2022-2031 [926]. Child care alone is 71% of that title [925]. The first stage is assumed to cost half of a near-universal design. Grown 4.5% a year to the ARA window. Ramp: 40% in FY2030 (supply grants first), 90% in FY2031. Low: CBO-style limited state participation. High: full participation and higher take-up."
P15b,P15,Childcare expansion to near-universal (FSW Act Title I),core,cost,Mandatory,II,Family and children,200.6,267.4,361.0,134.4,High,Data M Â· Model M Â· Behaviour H,814;925;926,"The remaining half of the same CBO-based cost, effective for tax years after 2031 (FY2033), half-year first year. The bill's capacity check may delay it; see the timing sensitivity. Pay-as-you-go: this line and the children's accounts may spend only what the billionaire tax, loophole closures, and digital levy bring in that year (FSW Act Sec. 5); any shortfall delays the phase-in rather than adding to the deficit."
P16,P16,"Children's investment accounts, progressive top-up (FSW Act Title V)",core,cost,Mandatory,II,Family and children,231.2,577.9,780.1,297.2,Very high,Data H Â· Model M Â· Behaviour M,942,"Amounts are not yet set in the bill, so the line is sized to the Booker baby-bond design, which CRFB estimated at about $60B a year in 2019 [942], scaled to deposits for ages 1-16 and grown 3% a year. Low: newborn deposits only. High: larger deposits than the Booker design. Subject to the same pay-as-you-go limit as the childcare expansion (FSW Act Sec. 5)."
P93c,P93,Paid family and medical leave benefits (WFA Title II),core,cost,Mandatory,II,Family and children,503.0,591.7,680.5,299.6,Medium,Data L Â· Model M Â· Behaviour M,815,"CBO: FAMILY Act benefits of $547B over 2020-2030, paid over about nine benefit years [815]. Grown with average wages (4%). Shown gross; the dedicated contribution is a separate revenue line below."
P19b,P19,Coverage bridge: restore enhanced premium tax credits (COLT Act Title IV),core,cost,Mandatory,I,Health,387.2,420.8,454.5,269.4,Medium,Data L Â· Model M Â· Behaviour M,813;320,CBO/JCT: $349.8B over 2026-2035 for permanent extension [813]. Shifted to the ARA window with health-cost growth (5%). FY2030 counts three quarters (credits restart in January 2030).
P23,P23,"Rural Health Transformation Program, made permanent (COLT Act Title IV)",core,cost,Mandatory,I,Health,90.0,90.0,101.6,60.0,Low,Data L Â· Model L Â· Behaviour L,454,"$10B a year, the current-law level, for each year after current funding ends in FY2030 [454]. High: indexed at 3%."
P111,P111,"Indian Health Service full funding, phased (TSTR Act Title I)",core,cost,Mandatory,I,Health,278.2,556.4,943.0,256.1,High,Data H Â· Model M Â· Behaviour L,827,"Gap between the tribal workgroup's FY2028 recommendation ($76.1B) and FY2026 enacted funding ($8.05B), about $68B a year [827], grown 5%. Central phases in evenly to full funding in FY2039. Low: phase to half the gap. High: full funding from FY2030. The source is a consultation output (tier C); the final figure is set in consultation."
P14,P14,Public health infrastructure (S-3),core,cost,Appropriation,I,Health,5.1,15.0,25.0,10.5,Medium,Data M Â· Model L Â· Behaviour L,,"Restoring CDC and vaccine programs, about $0.5-2.5B a year, subject to appropriation."
P112,P112,Truth and Healing Commission (TSTR Act Title II),core,cost,Appropriation,I,Health,0.1,0.1,0.1,0.1,Low,Data L Â· Model L Â· Behaviour L,446,"CBO estimate for S.761, about $90M in total [446]."
P7,P7,Wage floor indexing (ECFA Title I),core,cost,Mandatory,I,Wages and stabilizers,5.9,58.6,76.1,38.5,High,Data L Â· Model M Â· Behaviour H,816,"The starting wage is not yet set in the bill. Central assumes a start near $17, the level CBO scored in the Raise the Wage Act of 2023: +$46B conventional over 2024-2033 [816], shifted to the window at 4%. Low: indexing only. CBO's dynamic estimate is not included here."
P61,P61,"Restore wind and solar credits (IIC Act, Phase II stage)",core,cost,Revenue loss,II,Infrastructure and energy,136.4,209.8,335.6,99.8,High,Data M Â· Model M Â· Behaviour H,927;825,"JCT: the 2025 law's cuts to the 45Y and 48E clean electricity credits save about $152B over FY2026-2033 [927]. Restoration is costed at that rate, growing 8% a year with the build-out. High: Resources for the Future's larger estimate [825]."
P66,P66,Finish air traffic control modernization (IIC Act Title III),core,cost,Appropriation,I,Infrastructure and energy,18.9,30.0,30.0,30.0,Medium,Data M Â· Model L Â· Behaviour L,,"DOT's stated remaining need, $19B (July 2025) to $30B (September 2026 request), spread over five years."
P87,P87,Water infrastructure fund (IIC Act Title V),core,cost,Appropriation,I,Infrastructure and energy,82.3,98.0,107.8,68.6,Medium,Data L Â· Model L Â· Behaviour L,823,"Continues FY2026 infrastructure-law levels: about $8.2B a year for the state revolving funds and lead lines, plus $1.6B a year to replace earmarked funds [823]. High adds emerging contaminants."
P28,P28,Local news sustainability (COLT Act Title VI),core,cost,Appropriation,I,Other programs,5.0,11.0,17.1,7.7,Medium,Data M Â· Model M Â· Behaviour M,,"Analog: the former public broadcasting appropriation, about $1.1B a year. The journalism credit itself is unscored."
P69,P69,Cyber defense: restore CISA staffing,core,cost,Appropriation,I,Security and diplomacy,3.0,5.0,8.0,3.5,Medium,Data L Â· Model L Â· Behaviour L,,Reverses the proposed FY2027 cut of about $0.5B a year.
P75,P75,UN peacekeeping: clear arrears and pay assessments,core,cost,Appropriation,II,Security and diplomacy,6.4,12.8,19.1,8.8,Medium,Data L Â· Model L Â· Behaviour M,,"US arrears of $4.3B ($2.04B regular, $2.25B peacekeeping, April 2026) paid in the first year, then about $1.3B a year in assessments."
P82,P82,Climate preparedness and pre-disaster mitigation (IIC Act Title VI),core,cost,Appropriation,I,Infrastructure and energy,5.0,10.0,15.0,7.0,Medium,Data L Â· Model M Â· Behaviour L,,"Analog: the BRIC pre-disaster mitigation program, about $1B a year."
P58,P58,Teacher minimum salary grants (COLT Act Title V),bench,cost,Appropriation,I,Education and workforce,44.6,135.0,445.5,90.0,Very high,Data H Â· Model H Â· Behaviour M,940,"The amount is not yet set and no CBO score exists. Grants pay only the gap to the floor in districts that cannot meet it, so the central case assumes about $15B a year at full operation. High: about $50B a year, the size of an across-the-board federal raise proposed in 2023 [940]."
P54,P54,Tuition reduction and adult education (FSW Act Title II),bench,cost,Appropriation,I,Education and workforce,57.3,85.5,106.9,58.5,High,Data M Â· Model M Â· Behaviour M,939,"Analog: the 2023 federal free community college proposal, about $90B over ten years [939]."
P13,P13,National dual apprenticeship pipeline (COLT Act Title V),bench,cost,Appropriation,I,Education and workforce,6.0,9.0,19.8,6.3,Medium,Data M Â· Model L Â· Behaviour M,941,"Analog: CBO's estimate for the National Apprenticeship Act of 2020, about $0.6-0.8B a year assuming appropriation [941], plus the apprentice wage credit. High allows a larger credit."
P94,P94,Federal paid sick leave standard (WFA Title III),bench,cost,Appropriation,I,Education and workforce,0.5,1.0,3.0,0.7,Medium,Data L Â· Model L Â· Behaviour M,928,"CBO: the Healthy Families Act costs the federal budget about $0.1B a year (federal employees and enforcement) [928]. Its main cost falls on employers as a mandate, not on the budget."
P64,P64,Strategic domestic technology investment (IIC Act Title II),bench,cost,Appropriation,I,Infrastructure and energy,20.0,50.0,100.0,35.0,High,Data M Â· Model M Â· Behaviour M,938,"Analog: the CHIPS Act appropriated about $52.7B over five years [938]; the central case assumes half that pace, set by appropriation."
P89,P89,Diplomatic restoration and development finance,bench,cost,Appropriation,I,Security and diplomacy,5.0,10.0,20.0,7.0,Medium,Data M Â· Model L Â· Behaviour L,,"Analog: restoring Millennium Challenge Corporation and development finance levels, about $1B a year."
P24,P24,EMS stabilization grants (COLT Act Title IV),bench,cost,Appropriation,I,Health,2.0,5.0,15.0,3.5,High,Data H Â· Model M Â· Behaviour L,,The grant amount is not yet set; the central case assumes $0.5B a year. Ambulance billing protections mainly regulate private charges.
SMALL,Several,Allowance for small items (listed below),bench,cost,Appropriation,I,Other programs,20.0,40.0,120.0,28.0,High,Data H Â· Model M Â· Behaviour L,,"A placeholder of $4B a year for items each likely under $1B a year: voting holiday, free voter IDs and state grants, free firearm certificates, federal inspectors, USPS electrification, agricultural electrification, Pentagon audit, FDA reform, the MMIW office, and the S-1, S-2, S-3 and S-5 vehicles. High allows for a larger NASA and Space Force expansion."
P1,P1,"IRS enforcement and statutory Direct File, net of cost (COLT Act Title I)",core,revenue,Revenue,I,Revenue,60.0,125.0,450.0,75.1,High,Data M Â· Model M Â· Behaviour H,308;309;311;4,"Central uses CBO's net return on enforcement funding, about $125B a decade [308, 309], with a hiring ramp because the IRS lost much of its workforce in 2025-26 [311]. CBO counts only direct enforcement revenue. Low: capacity rebuilds slowly. High: estimates that include deterrence, as Treasury's do [4]."
P4,P4,Close wealth-accumulation loopholes (BWDS Act Title III),core,revenue,Revenue,II,Revenue,184.2,283.4,325.9,143.5,High,Data M Â· Model M Â· Behaviour H,821,"Treasury Greenbook: capital income reform about $289B, estate, gift and trust reforms $97.2B, carried interest $6.6B; $392.8B over 2025-2034 [821]. Reduced 15% for the changed baseline and grown 4%. Scored before the billionaire income tax (stacking order)."
P5,P5,Billionaire income tax (BWDS Act Title II),core,revenue,Revenue,II,Revenue,191.4,335.0,622.1,169.6,Very high,Data M Â· Model H Â· Behaviour H Â· Legal M,930;931;316,"The bill taxes billionaires' gains each year as income (mark-to-market on traded assets, a deferral charge on other assets, and tax at death), the design JCT scored at $557B over 2022â€“2031 [930]; Penn Wharton found $507B [931]. Reduced 10% for overlap with the loophole closures, grown 4% a year, and weighted for legal risk at even odds, since four Justices in Moore signaled that realization is required: if a court strikes the annual inclusion, Sec. 204(f) keeps the deferral charge and tax at death, assumed to keep 40% of revenue within the window. Low: fallback only. High: upheld, with a rate above the capital gains rate."
P93r,P93,"Paid leave dedicated contribution, set to cover the program (WFA Title II Sec. 203)",core,revenue,Revenue,II,Revenue,532.5,591.7,609.5,299.6,Low,Data L Â· Model L Â· Behaviour M,815,"Sec. 203 sets the contribution each year at the rate the Social Security Chief Actuary certifies will cover projected benefits and administration, so the program pays for itself, as Social Security does. Shown equal to the benefit line above. Low: a year's lag in adjusting the rate."
P5d,P5,Digital services levy (BWDS Act Title I),core,revenue,Revenue,II,Revenue,23.7,47.4,94.7,23.2,High,Data H Â· Model M Â· Behaviour M,943,"The rate is not yet set. The UK's 2% levy was projected to raise about Â£0.4B a year and France's about â‚¬0.5B [943]; scaled to a U.S. digital market roughly ten times larger, a 2â€“3% levy raises about $6B a year, growing 6%. High: a 3% rate on a broader base. Low: avoidance and narrow thresholds."
MAC,P19,Medicare Advantage coding accuracy: minimum adjustment raised from 5.9% to 7% in 2030 and 8% from 2031 (COLT Act Sec. 405),core,revenue,Mandatory savings,I,Revenue,193.4,257.9,296.5,158.0,Medium,Data L Â· Model M Â· Behaviour M,944;945,"CBO option: raising the coding-intensity adjustment to 8% saves about $200B over 2026â€“2035 [944, 945]. Phased in: about half the full saving in 2030 (7%), full from 2031 (8%). Grown 7% a year with Medicare Advantage spending. Insurers' overstated diagnoses pay for the coverage bridge; beneficiaries' benefits and premiums are unchanged by law, though plans may trim extras."
SNP,P23,"Site-neutral Medicare payment for off-campus outpatient services, rural hospitals exempt (COLT Act Sec. 406)",core,revenue,Mandatory savings,I,Revenue,126.2,180.3,198.4,114.1,Medium,Data L Â· Model M Â· Behaviour M,944;945,"CBO option: paying the same rate for most services whether delivered in a hospital outpatient department or a physician's office saves about $180B over 2026â€“2035 [944, 945]. Reduced 15% for the rural exemption, grown 5%, half-year first year. Patients' coinsurance falls with the payment."
CORP,COLT,Corporate income tax rate from 21% to 23% (COLT Act Sec. 304),core,revenue,Revenue,I,Revenue,254.9,318.7,334.6,205.9,Medium,Data L Â· Model M Â· Behaviour M,944;946,"CBO option: each percentage point on the corporate rate raises about $140B over 2026â€“2035 [944, 946], so two points raise about $280B. Grown with GDP; 60% in FY2030 because the rate starts with tax years after 2029. Low: more profit shifting and interaction with the 2025 law's international rules, which CBO's estimate predates. Fallback at 22%: about half."
P19p,P19,Public option savings (POHE Act),core,revenue,Mandatory savings,II,Revenue,0.0,73.5,242.5,36.6,Very high,Data H Â· Model H Â· Behaviour M,57;319,CBO (2013): a public plan saves $158B over ten years [57]. Grown with health costs and cut by 70% because the estimate is old and later CBO work ties savings to payment rates [319]. High: the full updated estimate.
P73,P73,Military contractor savings (executive),zero,revenue,Discretionary savings,I,Revenue,0.0,0.0,100.0,0.0,Very high,No specified mechanism,,"No mechanism is specified and no scorekeeper has estimated it, so nothing is counted. The high case shows $100B to mark the potential."
S6,P21,"Payroll tax on earnings above $250,000 (S-6)",trust,revenue,Trust fund (off-budget),I,Social Security,1622.1,1802.4,1892.5,1153.8,Low,Data L Â· Model L Â· Behaviour M,811;810;808,"CBO Budget Option: $1,426.8B over 2025-2034 [811], shifted to the window at 5% a year (earnings above the cap grow faster than wages overall). Assumed to apply to wages paid from January 2030. Credited only to the Social Security trust funds."

